<article class="env-lemma" id="00EM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00EM">Lemma <span data-tag="00EM">10.24.3</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. If $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) = U \amalg V$ with both $U$ and $V$ open then $R \cong R_1 \times R_2$ with $U \cong \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R_1)$ and $V \cong \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R_2)$ via the maps in Lemma <a href="/tag/00ED" data-tag="00ED">10.21.2</a>. Moreover, both $R_1$ and $R_2$ are localizations as well as quotients of the ring $R$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/00EE" data-tag="00EE">10.21.3</a> we have $U = D(e)$ and $V = D(1-e)$ for some idempotent $e$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00EJ" data-tag="00EJ">10.24.2</a> we see that $R \cong R_ e \times R_{1 - e}$ (since clearly $R_{e(1-e)} = 0$ so the glueing condition is trivial; of course it is trivial to prove the product decomposition directly in this case). The lemma follows. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>